    ('local-immigration', 1, 'Teater''s reelection campaign explicitly listed ''codifying Sanctuary City status'' and ''joining four lawsuits against the federal government to keep Beaverton families safe'' as council accomplishments. He voted for the January 2026 sanctuary ordinance update prohibiting city employees from sharing immigration data with federal agencies except where legally required. His only stated concern was procedural (amendment language should have been reviewed by city attorney first), not substantive opposition to the policy''s protections.', ARRAY['https://beavertonvalleytimes.com/2026/04/21/kevin-teater-seeks-reelection-to-beaverton-city-council/', 'https://beavertonvalleytimes.com/2026/01/27/beaverton-hones-sanctuary-city-code-preventing-city-employees-from-helping-ice-officials/', 'https://katu.com/news/local/beaverton-mayor-lacey-beaty-councilors-nadia-hasan-kevin-teater-considers-having-officers-investigate-federal-immigration-ice-agents']::text[]),
    ('civil-rights', 2, 'In his 2022 candidate Q&A, Teater identified racial equity as one of his top three issues and stated he had been ''pushing council to challenge our thinking when it comes to housing policy to dismantle past racist systems.'' He also noted that urban renewal history ''is very racist, classist, and destructive'' and that BURA must keep ''equity and racial reconciliation as top priorities.'' He supports small-business programs specifically targeting ''immigrants, refugees, and people of color'' to reduce barriers to entrepreneurship.', ARRAY['https://beavertonvalleytimes.com/2022/04/28/qa-get-to-know-beaverton-city-council-candidates/', 'https://pamplinmedia.com/bvt/15-news/543526-435063-q-and-a-get-to-know-beaverton-city-council-candidates']::text[]),
